Part A: the anatomy of a function
Roughly 75 minutes. The first 25 teach what a function is; the rest apply it to the running example, turning a messy script into organised, tested code.
R lets you define your own functions with function(). The shape is always the same: a name, some arguments, a body, and a returned value.
my_function &lt;- function(arg1, arg2) {
  # what the function does, in a comment
  result &lt;- arg1 + arg2
  return(result)
}
A minimal example:
add_one &lt;- function(x) {
  return(x + 1)
}

add_one(10)   # 11
add_one(c(1, 5, 10))   # 2 6 11 -- it vectorises, because R does

Part D: the function factory
It’s functions all the way down…
A function factory is a function that builds and returns other functions. You give the factory some configuration, and it hands back a new function with that configuration baked in and ready to use. If you have ever written nearly identical unit converters, threshold checkers, or formatters and felt that the repetition was unnecessary, a function factory lets you collapse those variants into a single recipe.
The mechanism rests on lexical scoping. When a function is defined inside another function, it keeps a reference to the environment in which it was created. So if the outer function had a variable called factor, the inner function can still see it long after the outer call has finished. That preserved environment is called a closure, and it is what allows the manufactured functions to remember the settings you gave them. In practice, you write the factory once and produce as many specialised functions as you need:

# The factory: takes a multiplier, returns a function configured with it
make_converter &lt;- function(factor) {
  function(x) x * factor
}

# Manufacture two specialised converters from the same factory
g_to_kg  &lt;- make_converter(1 / 1000)
mm_to_cm &lt;- make_converter(1 / 10)

penguins_clean |&gt;
  drop_na(body_mass_g, bill_length_mm) |&gt;
  mutate(
    body_mass_kg   = g_to_kg(body_mass_g),
    bill_length_cm = mm_to_cm(bill_length_mm)
  ) |&gt;
  select(species, body_mass_kg, bill_length_cm)

The point to take away is that g_to_kg() and mm_to_cm() are distinct functions, each carrying its own value of factor, bound at the moment the factory was called. That persistent enclosing environment is the closure. A function factory is simply a function that uses it to produce configured functions on demand. This is useful when you want to avoid repeating yourself across many similar transformations, or to pre-configure behaviour (a threshold, a unit, a reference mean) once and reuse it.

Part B: iteration on the running example
Recall the list-column from Block 2: group_by(species) |&gt; nest() puts each species’ tibble into one cell of a data column.

species_data &lt;- penguins_clean |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(species) |&gt;
  tidyr::nest()

species_data
# # A tibble: 3 x 2
# # Groups:   species [3]
#   species   data               
#   &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;list&gt;             
# 1 Adelie    &lt;tibble [151 x 16]&gt;
# 2 Gentoo    &lt;tibble [123 x 16]&gt;
# 3 Chinstrap &lt;tibble [68 x 16]&gt; 







Note



We use the classic group_by() |&gt; nest() rather than the newer nest(.by = species). Both nest the data the same way, but only the first leaves the result grouped by species (which is why we ungroup() before unnesting below). The explicit grouping step is clearer and backward-compatible.


Applying a function to each cell
Used inside mutate(), map() applies a function to each cell of a list-column and stores the result as a new list-column:

species_models &lt;- species_data |&gt;
  dplyr::mutate(
    model = purrr::map(data, \(d) lm(body_mass_g ~ flipper_length_mm, data = d)),
    coefs = purrr::map(model, \(m) broom::tidy(m, conf.int = TRUE)),
    n_obs = purrr::map_int(data, nrow)
  )

species_models
# # A tibble: 3 x 5
# # Groups:   species [3]
#   species   data                model  coefs            n_obs
#   &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;list&gt;              &lt;list&gt; &lt;list&gt;           &lt;int&gt;
# 1 Adelie    &lt;tibble [151 x 16]&gt; &lt;lm&gt;   &lt;tibble [2 x 7]&gt;   151
# 2 Gentoo    &lt;tibble [123 x 16]&gt; &lt;lm&gt;   &lt;tibble [2 x 7]&gt;   123
# 3 Chinstrap &lt;tibble [68 x 16]&gt;  &lt;lm&gt;   &lt;tibble [2 x 7]&gt;    68

Three things to notice. The model cell holds a fitted lm per species; the coefs cell holds a tidied tibble per species; and n_obs is an ordinary integer column, not a list-column, because map_int() returns an atomic vector. The typed variants slot straight into a tibble.
Unnesting back to a flat table
For a manuscript coefficient table, expand the coefs list-column back into rows. unnest() is the dual of nest(): each cell’s tibble becomes rows, with the surrounding columns repeated.

coefficient_table &lt;- species_models |&gt;
  dplyr::ungroup() |&gt;
  dplyr::select(species, coefs) |&gt;
  tidyr::unnest(coefs)

A real refactor: decompose the function
There is a tension. fit_species_model() filters and fits, but nest() has already done the filtering. Calling it inside the iteration would filter twice. That tension is information: the function is doing two jobs. Split them.

# R/functions/modelling_functions.R -- revised

#' Fit body mass ~ flipper length on pre-filtered data
#' @param data A data frame, assumed to be one species' worth.
#' @return A fitted lm object.
fit_model &lt;- function(data) {
  lm(body_mass_g ~ flipper_length_mm, data = data)
}

#' Tidy a fitted lm with confidence intervals
#' @param model A fitted lm object.
#' @param conf_level Confidence level. Default 0.95.
#' @return A tibble of tidied coefficients.
tidy_model &lt;- function(model, conf_level = 0.95) {
  broom::tidy(model, conf.int = TRUE, conf.level = conf_level)
}

#' Fit for one species (wrapper preserving the old interface)
#' @param data A data frame with a `species` column.
#' @param sp Character of length 1. Species to filter to.
#' @param conf_level Confidence level. Default 0.95.
fit_species_model &lt;- function(data, sp, conf_level = 0.95) {
  if (!sp %in% unique(data$species)) {
    stop("Species '", sp, "' is not in the data. ",
         "Available species: ",
         paste(sort(unique(data$species)), collapse = ", "))
  }
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::filter(species == sp) |&gt;
    fit_model() |&gt;
    tidy_model(conf_level = conf_level) |&gt;
    dplyr::mutate(species = sp, .before = 1)
}

fit_model() and tidy_model() now exist independently and compose. The old public interface survives as a wrapper, so existing callers and the tests from Block 3 still pass with no edits. The behaviour is unchanged and only the internals have improved, which is what a refactor should achieve.
The iteration, rewritten
With single-responsibility functions, the iteration uses them directly and reads cleanly:

species_models &lt;- penguins_clean |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(species) |&gt;
  tidyr::nest() |&gt;
  dplyr::mutate(
    model = purrr::map(data, fit_model),
    coefs = purrr::map(model, tidy_model)
  )

coefficient_table &lt;- species_models |&gt;
  dplyr::ungroup() |&gt;
  dplyr::select(species, coefs) |&gt;
  tidyr::unnest(coefs)

The modelling logic that began as three copy-pasted blocks in Block 3, and became three function calls by the end of that block, is now one pipeline that applies the same function to every group.
Activity: iterate over a different grouping


Iterate over a different grouping


The penguins data has an island column (“Biscoe”, “Dream”, “Torgersen”). Fit a separate body_mass_g ~ flipper_length_mm model for each island and produce a tidied coefficient table, using the nest pattern.




Solution


island_coefs &lt;- penguins_clean |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(island) |&gt;
  tidyr::nest() |&gt;
  dplyr::mutate(
    model = purrr::map(data, fit_model),
    coefs = purrr::map(model, tidy_model)
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::ungroup() |&gt;
  dplyr::select(island, coefs) |&gt;
  tidyr::unnest(coefs)

fit_model() and tidy_model() are reused unchanged. They never knew they would iterate over species; they work for islands too, because each has a single responsibility and no hidden assumption about the grouping.

Extension exercise: plots in a list-column


Extension exercise: plots in a list-column


A list-column can hold plots as readily as models. Using the nested data, add a plot column made with map() that holds one scatter plot per species, then draw them together with patchwork::wrap_plots(). Hint: build each plot inside \(d) ggplot(d, ...).




Solution


species_plots &lt;- penguins_clean |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(species) |&gt;
  tidyr::nest() |&gt;
  dplyr::mutate(
    plot = purrr::map(data, \(d)
      ggplot2::ggplot(d, ggplot2::aes(flipper_length_mm, body_mass_g)) +
        ggplot2::geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
        ggplot2::geom_smooth(method = "lm") +
        ggplot2::theme_minimal())
  )

patchwork::wrap_plots(species_plots$plot)

The same nest |&gt; mutate(map(...)) pattern that fits models also builds figures. Once you see a cell as “any R object”, models, plots and tables are all just list-columns.







Note



Where this leaves us. Copy-paste is gone. One pipeline fits a model per group, whatever the group is. But fit_model() still hardcodes the response and predictor: it only ever fits body mass on flipper length. make_scatter_plot() from Block 3 likewise only ever plots those two columns. Block 5 removes that last limitation from both.

Embracing in practice
The fix:

summarise_by_species &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

summarise_by_species(penguins_clean, body_mass_g)
# # A tibble: 3 x 2
#   species    mean
#   &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
# 1 Adelie    3701.
# 2 Gentoo    5076.
# 3 Chinstrap 3733.

Embrace the column every time it appears, and embrace the grouping column too if it is an argument:

summary_grouped &lt;- function(data, var, group) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(
      mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      sd   = sd({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      .by  = {{ group }}
    )
}

summary_grouped(penguins_clean, body_mass_g, species)
summary_grouped(penguins_clean, body_mass_g, island)







Note



Under the hood (optional). { var } is shorthand for !!enquo(var). rlang::enquo() captures the argument as a quosure (the expression plus the environment it came from), and !! injects it back into the verb. You almost never need to write enquo()/!! by hand, but seeing the expansion demystifies the curly braces: they capture, then inject.

Strings and dynamic names
When a column name arrives as a string, embracing does not apply: strings are values, not expressions to capture. Use the .data pronoun in data-masking contexts:

summarise_by_string &lt;- function(data, var_name) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean(.data[[var_name]], na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

summarise_by_string(penguins_clean, "body_mass_g")

For output column names built from an argument, = is not enough: with = the name is used literally, so "{{ var }}_mean" = … makes a column called { var }_mean. The walrus operator := lets rlang fill in { var }, using glue-style name injection:

summarise_dynamic &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(
      "{{ var }}_mean" := mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      "{{ var }}_sd"   := sd({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE),
      .by = species
    )
}

summarise_dynamic(penguins_clean, body_mass_g)
# # A tibble: 3 x 3
#   species   body_mass_g_mean body_mass_g_sd
#   &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;
# 1 Adelie               3701.           459.
# 2 Gentoo               5076.           504.
# 3 Chinstrap            3733.           384.

Two things happen on the left of :=. "{{ var }}_mean" is glue-style injection, replacing { var } with the argument’s name; := is the assignment that lets rlang do that injection (with = the name is used literally). Both are needed.

Choosing the pattern
The patterns above each answer one combination of two questions:


How does the column name arrive? Bare, as the user typed it; or as a string or character vector?

What kind of argument receives it? Data masking or tidy selection (check the help page)?



The name arrives as…
…into data masking
…into tidy selection



A bare name
{ var }
{ var }


A string
.data[[var]]
all_of(var)


A character vector
across(all_of(vars), f)
all_of(vars)



And for new output names: "{{ var }}_mean" := (bare) or "{var_name}_mean" := (string). Memorise the first row; the rest is reference.
Activity 6: choose the pattern


Activity 6: choose the pattern


For each scenario, name the correct pattern from the table, then write the function. (a), (b) and (d) group by species.

var arrives as a bare name; compute its mean (data masking).
var_name arrives as a string; compute its mean (data masking).
cols arrives as a character vector; select those columns (tidy selection).
var arrives as a bare name; compute its mean into an output column named &lt;var&gt;_mean (data masking with a new output name).





Solution


# (a) bare name, data masking -&gt; {{ }}
summarise_mean &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

# (b) string, data masking -&gt; .data[[ ]]
summarise_mean_str &lt;- function(data, var_name) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise(mean = mean(.data[[var_name]], na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

# (c) character vector, tidy selection -&gt; all_of()
select_cols &lt;- function(data, cols) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::select(species, dplyr::all_of(cols))
}

# (d) bare name, new output name -&gt; {{ }} with :=
summarise_named &lt;- function(data, var) {
  data |&gt;
    dplyr::summarise("{{ var }}_mean" := mean({{ var }}, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species)
}

The decision procedure: first ask whether the name arrives bare or as a string; a bare name is always { }. For a string or vector, ask what kind of argument receives it: data masking (.data[[ ]]) or tidy selection (all_of()).

type /clear before each exercise, so each starts a new conversation;
end each prompt with “Answer from what I’ve pasted: don’t read files, run code or edit anything.” Without it, Claude Code may gather evidence itself: when we tested Exercise 2’s prompt without that line, it searched the project and read both data files, including the one Block 7 asks you to explore yourself;
if it still asks to run a command or edit a file, choose No. Manual mode means it must ask first, and your answer is the control.









TipUsing Posit Assistant or a chat window instead





Posit Assistant: type /ask first. Ask mode is read-only: it cannot edit files or run R code, so the answer-only line is not needed. It does see the names and types of the objects in your session (not their values) and can read your console history, so it may already have your distinct() output: that is a disclosure route too (Block 4). For step 3 of Exercise 2, type /normal so it can run the diagnostics in your live R session, approving each call; /normal is also how you leave Ask mode.
A chat window: paste the same prompts, without the answer-only line, and skip step 3 of Exercise 2.

The loop, with no model at all
You can run this section without an API key. It replaces the model with a pretend one that follows a script, so that you can see the loop the harness runs. Each pass of repeat is one round.

# the tools, as plain R functions
tools &lt;- list(
  n_rows = function(name) {
    nrow(get(name, envir = as.environment("package:datasets")))
  }
)

# a pretend model: asks for a tool until it has two results, then answers
pretend_model &lt;- function(turns) {
  results &lt;- Filter(function(t) t$role == "tool result", turns)
  if (length(results) == 0) {
    list(role = "assistant", tool = "n_rows", args = list(name = "mtcars"))
  } else if (length(results) == 1) {
    list(role = "assistant", tool = "n_rows", args = list(name = "iris"))
  } else {
    list(role = "assistant",
         text = paste("iris has", results[[2]]$text, "rows and mtcars has",
                      results[[1]]$text, "so iris has more."))
  }
}

# the harness: send the conversation, run any tool asked for, repeat
question &lt;- "Which has more rows, mtcars or iris?"
turns &lt;- list(list(role = "user", text = question))
repeat {
  reply &lt;- pretend_model(turns)
  turns &lt;- c(turns, list(reply))
  if (is.null(reply$tool)) break # no tool asked for: this is the answer
  result &lt;- do.call(tools[[reply$tool]], reply$args)
  turns &lt;- c(turns, list(list(role = "tool result", text = result)))
}

for (t in turns) {
  what &lt;- if (is.null(t$tool)) t$text else
    paste0("call ", t$tool, "(", t$args, ")")
  cat(sprintf("%-12s %s\n", t$role, what))
}

user         Which has more rows, mtcars or iris?
assistant    call n_rows(mtcars)
tool result  32
assistant    call n_rows(iris)
tool result  150
assistant    iris has 150 rows and mtcars has 32 so iris has more.


Three things to notice:


The model only asks. do.call() in the harness is the only line that does anything to your computer. Every control in the course acts at that line: Manual mode asks you before it runs, a deny rule refuses it, a hook inspects it.

Results go back to the model. Whatever a tool returns joins the conversation, and the conversation is sent to the provider. That is Block 4’s disclosure route: a tool that reads a data file sends that data.

The model decides when to stop. The loop ends when a reply asks for no tool. A real model can also loop for longer than you expected, which is why a real harness has limits and a stop key.

In the real API the tool result goes back inside a user turn; ellmer does that for you.

G.4 Driving data.table with dplyr: dtplyr

With dtplyr, you wrap a data.table in lazy_dt(), write the dplyr you already know, and call as_tibble() (or collect()) at the end to trigger execution and return a normal tibble.

library(dtplyr)

allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1988) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE),
            .by = species_id) |&gt;
  as_tibble()

The lazy_dt() step is the important change from older tutorials. Evaluation is lazy: the pipeline builds up a translation but runs nothing until as_tibble()/collect(). To see the data.table code that dtplyr generates, pipe into show_query().

allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1988) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species_id) |&gt;
  show_query()







Tip



Piping a bare data.table straight into a dplyr verb does work, but not in the way you might hope: there is no automatic conversion to lazy_dt() and no warning. Because a data.table is also a data.frame, dplyr simply runs its ordinary data-frame methods, so you get dplyr speed rather than data.table speed. Make the lazy_dt() step explicit; it is what switches on the translation.


There is a small, fixed translation overhead per verb (under a millisecond), which is irrelevant for large data but means dtplyr is not worth it for tiny tables. And because dtplyr preserves dplyr’s copy-on-modify semantics, it is marginally slower than hand-written data.table. For most analysis work that trade is well worth the readability.

G.4.1 Activity: translate a pipeline


the same question, two ways


Using allData, find the mean hindfoot_length per species_id for records after 1985, sorted from largest to smallest mean. Write it (a) as native data.table, (b) as a dtplyr pipeline, then use show_query() to compare your dtplyr code with the data.table it generates. Are they the same?




Solution


# (a) native data.table
allData[year &gt; 1985,
        .(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE)),
        by = species_id][order(-mean_hf)]

# (b) dtplyr
allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1985) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species_id) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(mean_hf)) |&gt;
  as_tibble()

# inspect the translation
allData |&gt;
  lazy_dt() |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 1985) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_hf = mean(hindfoot_length, na.rm = TRUE), .by = species_id) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(mean_hf)) |&gt;
  show_query()

The generated query is close to the hand-written version. It will not be identical character for character, because dtplyr introduces intermediate steps to honour dplyr semantics, but it uses the same [i, j, by] machinery, which is where the speed comes from.

The loop
An agentAn assistant that can read across a project’s files and, depending on its permission mode, edit them and run commands, such as Claude Code. An agent acts; a chat window only advises. repeats that exchange until a reply asks for no tool. Wickham’s definition: “An agent is an LLM, in a harness, that calls tools repeatedly in a loop, deciding each next step from the last result.” Each time, the harnessThe software around a model that keeps the conversation, sends it to the model, runs the tools the model asks for and sends back their results. Claude Code is a harness; so is a chat object made with ellmer. sends the conversation, runs any tool the model asks for, adds the result, and sends it all again. Every control in this course acts on one of three facts:


The model only asks. The harness runs each tool on your computer, so that is where Manual mode asks you, a deny rule refuses, and a hook inspects.

Results go back to the model. A tool that reads a data file sends that data to the provider: Block 3’s disclosure route.

The model decides when to stop. It can loop longer than you expect. That is why a real harness has limits on how long it may run, and a stop key that interrupts it mid-loop: Esc in Claude Code (Block 1). In R, you interrupt chat$chat() as you would any long-running code: Esc in the RStudio console, or Ctrl+C in a terminal. Your mini agent sets no limit of its own; your approvals are its brake.







